            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Literary Contributions</strong><br>\nZola's literary output was vast, consisting of over 20 novels, several short stories, and numerous essays. He is best known for his Les Rougon-Macquart series, a sequence of 20 novels that follow a family during the Second French Empire. This body of work exemplifies his style of naturalism, where he investigated the impact of environment and heredity on human behavior.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Zola's Naturalism</strong><br>\nZola is often regarded as the father of naturalism, a literary movement that began in the late 19th century. Naturalism extends the concept of realism, aiming to present a more scientific and objective perspective. Zola believed that a novelist's role was similar to that of a scientist, studying human behavior under controlled conditions and portraying life without romanticization or idealization.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Society</strong><br>\nZola's work had a considerable impact on French society. His novels exposed the harsh realities of life for the working class and the corrupt nature of the French political system. Through his writing, Zola influenced public opinion on various social issues, including the plight of the poor, the treatment of women, and the need for political reform.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
