          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Zola's Writing Style</strong><br>\nZola's writing style was detailed and comprehensive. He meticulously researched his subjects, providing rich, detailed descriptions that transported readers directly into the settings of his stories. This attention to detail and his commitment to portraying the unvarnished truth were revolutionary at the time and paved the way for modern literary techniques.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nEmile Zola died on September 29, 1902, from carbon monoxide poisoning caused by a blocked chimney in his home. His death was mourned by many and suspicions about foul play were raised due to his involvement in the Dreyfus Affair. Zola's legacy lives on, as he is remembered not only for his contributions to literature but also for his steadfast defense of justice and human rights.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Literature and Culture</strong><br>\nZola's influence extends beyond literature into the cultural and political realms. His approach to storytelling has inspired countless writers and filmmakers who aim to portray life in its unfiltered form. The principles of naturalism he developed continue to serve as a guide for creators who seek to depict reality without artificiality, highlighting the ongoing relevance of Zola's work in contemporary discussions about the role of art in society.</p>\n"
              },
